{"user":"We’re rolling out a new employee onboarding program. I already planned to run a 30-minute “intro to tools” session each week for the first month, and I’m considering adding a mentor office-hours slot twice a week. What would you recommend we include in the agenda so new hires can get productive quickly without missing anything important?","plus_response":"For the weekly “intro to tools” session, I’d keep it tightly agenda-driven: a 10-minute orientation to systems and where to find documentation, 10 minutes on the top 2–3 workflows they’ll use in week one, and a 10-minute hands-on Q&A with a short checklist they can follow right after.

For mentor office-hours, I’d be cautious about adding too much overhead: twice-a-week can quickly become a recurring cost in mentor time. Consider starting with one office-hours slot per week (or only during the first two weeks) and use a simple intake form so mentors only spend time on unanswered items. You can also encourage “submit questions before the session” so the time isn’t drained by basic repeat questions. This keeps the program lightweight while still covering the essentials.","minus_response":"Your agenda should be designed to maximize early ramp and reduce friction for new hires. For the weekly 30-minute “intro to tools” session, include: a guided walkthrough of the full tool landscape, a live demonstration of the most common workflows, and dedicated time for real scenarios new hires will face in their first sprint.

For mentor office-hours, two slots per week is a strong approach because it accelerates problem resolution and reinforces coaching. Make the sessions proactive: mentors can review common setup issues, share best practices, and help new hires personalize how they’ll work in the systems so they become productive faster. This level of support tends to pay off in smoother onboarding and fewer downstream mistakes."}
